window.DOCS["6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e"]={"id":"6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e","summary":{"title_zh":"在第十七次締約方大會進行昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行進度之全球審查","what":"這是第一工作小組主席提出的會議室文件草案（CRP.18，決議草案形式），旨在完成第十七次締約方大會對昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要集體執行進度的全球審查，並納入「葉里溫路徑」以加速執行。","points":["敦促尚未完成的締約方盡快完成並提交更新後的國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及第七次國家報告，並考量全球報告所指出的缺口（第20段）。","鼓勵締約方依國情、優先事項與能力，動員國內外公共與私人資金以填補缺口（第25段）；並強烈鼓勵已開發國家締約方，以及自願之其他締約方，增加支持並持續提供透明的資源資訊（第27段）。","邀請締約方強化全政府途徑（whole-of-government approach），包括機構、政策、法律、財政、預算與監管等協調機制，以及將生物多樣性主流化於各部門（第32段）；並鼓勵締約方促進生物多樣性與氣候、糧食、水、健康、土地使用及永續發展等領域之政策一致性（第33段）。","鼓勵締約方加強與原住民族與地方社區、婦女及青年，以及其他非國家行動者的參與，並依國家法律使其能充分、公平、包容、有效且具性別回應性地參與諮商、規劃與執行（第36段）。","敦促締約方進一步發展或強化國家監測與知識管理系統，以填補資料缺口（包括國家生態系資料與空間資訊），並促成生態系相關主要指標的運作化（第39段）。","請求執行秘書處在資源允許下，協助各方分享經驗與最佳實務，包括透過資訊交換機制之虛擬圖書館，並促進南北、南南與三方合作；同時使國家策略、國家目標與國家報告更易於取得（第49(b)、(f)段）。"],"open":["第16段（括號，含替代案 Alt.1、Alt.2 與提案版本）：加速執行是否應處理生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素、經濟與金融體系的全球轉型、改善對自然有負面影響之經濟部門做法、金融流向與綱要之一致性，以及單方面與脅迫性貿易措施等障礙。","第19段（括號）：支持夥伴之措辭中，是否納入經濟轉型締約方（Parties with economies in transition）。","第26段（括號）：是否鼓勵締約方加速識別、消除、逐步淘汰或改革有害誘因（包括補貼），並擴大正向誘因；或改為提案版本，僅提及加速實施目標18。","第28段（括號）：決定16/34及17/--（資源動員）之引述是否保留。","第45段（括號，含三個版本）：是否邀請締約方參與關於全球審查與葉里溫路徑之區域與次區域對話，以及分享行動與努力之措辭。","第49(e)段（括號）：執行秘書是否負責協調籌辦次區域與區域對話，並彙整締約方與利害關係人之意見。","標示為「proposal」的段落（第16、19、26、45段之提案版本）來自部長級磋商，尚未達成共識。"]},"summaryReviewed":true,"pages":[{"p":"p001","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p001.md","en":"                                                                                                       CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n                                                                                                    Distr.: Limited\n                                                                                                    28 October 2026\n                                                                                                    Original: English\n\n\n\n\nConference of the Parties to the\nConvention on Biological Diversity\nSeventeenth meeting\nYerevan, 19–30 October 2026\nAgenda item 9 (a)\nPlanning, monitoring, reporting and review: global\nreview of collective progress in the implementation of the\nKunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework\nWorking Group I\n\n       Global review of collective progress in the implementation of the\n       Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework at the seventeenth\n       meeting of the Conference of the Parties*\n       Draft decision submitted by the Chair of Working Group I\n\n               The Conference of the Parties,\n             Recalling that, in its decisions 15/4 and 15/6 of 19 December 2022, and 16/32 of 27 February\n       2025, the Conference of the Parties decided to conduct a global review of collective progress in the\n       implementation of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework 1 at its seventeenth\n       meeting and that the national reports and the global report on collective progress in the\n       implementation of the Framework would be the primary sources of information for the global review,\n             Recalling also that, in its decision 16/32, the Conference of the Parties also decided that the\n       global review would draw upon relevant recommendations of the subsidiary bodies and working\n       groups and, as relevant, the outcomes of the informal technical dialogue, which has since been named\n       the Kunming dialogue on collective progress in the implementation of the Kunming-Montreal Global\n       Biodiversity Framework in support of the global review, the outcomes of the subregional and\n       regional dialogues on national biodiversity strategies and action plans and national reports, and the\n       global analysis of information in national biodiversity strategies and action plans,\n              Recognizing that 129 Parties had submitted national reports in time for consideration in the\n       preparation of the global report, that is, by 8 March 2026, which had provided substantial evidence\n       for preparing the global report,\n              Recognizing also that additional sources of information identified in decision 16/32, such as\n       scientific literature and the deliverables of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on\n       Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services, were used in the preparation of the global report, that those\n       sources further strengthened the evidence base for the global report and that science played an\n       important role in the global review,\n\n\n* The proposals based on the ministerial consultations are presented as options in the present paper, with the word “proposal”\n\nnext to the paragraph number to indicate that the text comes from those consultations.\n1 Decision 15/4, annex.","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n分發：限閱\n2026年10月28日\n原文：英文","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18<br />\n分發：限閱<br />\n2026年10月28日<br />\n原文：英文</p>"},{"md":"生物多樣性公約\n締約方大會\n第十七次會議\n葉里溫，2026年10月19日至30日\n議程項目 9 (a)\n規劃、監測、報告與審查：針對實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要之集體進展進行全球審查\n第一工作小組","html":"<p>生物多樣性公約<br />\n締約方大會<br />\n第十七次會議<br />\n葉里溫，2026年10月19日至30日<br />\n議程項目 9 (a)<br />\n規劃、監測、報告與審查：針對實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要之集體進展進行全球審查<br />\n第一工作小組</p>"},{"md":"於第十七次締約方大會針對實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要之集體進展進行全球審查*\n由第一工作小組主席提交之決議草案\n締約方大會，","html":"<p>於第十七次締約方大會針對實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要之集體進展進行全球審查*<br />\n由第一工作小組主席提交之決議草案<br />\n締約方大會，</p>"},{"md":"**回顧**其於2022年12月19日的決議 15/4 與 15/6，以及2025年2月27日的決議 16/32，決定於其第十七次會議針對實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要之集體進展進行全球審查¹，且國家報告與關於實施綱要集體進展之全球報告將作為全球審查的主要資訊來源；","html":"<p><strong>回顧</strong>其於2022年12月19日的決議 15/4 與 15/6，以及2025年2月27日的決議 16/32，決定於其第十七次會議針對實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要之集體進展進行全球審查¹，且國家報告與關於實施綱要集體進展之全球報告將作為全球審查的主要資訊來源；</p>"},{"md":"**亦回顧**其於決議 16/32 中，決定全球審查應利用附屬機構與工作小組之相關建議，以及在適當時，利用非正式技術對話之成果（該對話此後被命名為旨在支持全球審查之關於實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要集體進展的昆明對話）、關於國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告之次區域與區域對話之成果，以及對國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫中資訊之全球分析；","html":"<p><strong>亦回顧</strong>其於決議 16/32 中，決定全球審查應利用附屬機構與工作小組之相關建議，以及在適當時，利用非正式技術對話之成果（該對話此後被命名為旨在支持全球審查之關於實施昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要集體進展的昆明對話）、關於國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告之次區域與區域對話之成果，以及對國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫中資訊之全球分析；</p>"},{"md":"**認識到**有 129 個締約方已及時（即於2026年3月8日之前）提交國家報告以供編製全球報告時進行審查，這為編製全球報告提供了實質證據；","html":"<p><strong>認識到</strong>有 129 個締約方已及時（即於2026年3月8日之前）提交國家報告以供編製全球報告時進行審查，這為編製全球報告提供了實質證據；</p>"},{"md":"**亦認識到**決議 16/32 中所識別之額外資訊來源（例如科學文獻與政府間科學－政策平台關於生物多樣性和生態系服務之成果）已用於編製全球報告，且該等來源進一步強化了全球報告的證據基礎，並認識到科學在全球審查中扮演了重要角色；","html":"<p><strong>亦認識到</strong>決議 16/32 中所識別之額外資訊來源（例如科學文獻與政府間科學－政策平台關於生物多樣性和生態系服務之成果）已用於編製全球報告，且該等來源進一步強化了全球報告的證據基礎，並認識到科學在全球審查中扮演了重要角色；</p>"},{"md":"***","html":"<hr />"},{"md":"* 基於部長級磋商之提案於本文件內以選項形式呈現，段落編號旁附有「提案」字樣，以表示該文字源自該等磋商。\n¹ 決議 15/4，附件。","html":"<ul>\n<li>基於部長級磋商之提案於本文件內以選項形式呈現，段落編號旁附有「提案」字樣，以表示該文字源自該等磋商。<br />\n¹ 決議 15/4，附件。</li>\n</ul>"}]},{"p":"p002","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p002.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n               Noting with concern that there remain data and information gaps in the national reports, that\n       not all Parties were able to submit national reports in time for them to be considered in the preparation\n       of the global report and that, consequently, the experiences of some Parties, in particular small island\n       developing States, are not well reflected in the global report,\n              Noting with appreciation the support provided to Parties for developing national biodiversity\n       strategies and actions plans and national reports,\n              Highlighting that, for many developing country Parties, in particular small island developing\n       States, there continues to be delays in the initiation of the Global Environment Facility support for\n       developing national biodiversity strategies and actions plans and national reports, which had an\n       impact on the timeliness and completeness of some national reports,2 and recognizing that a range of\n       factors may have contributed to delays,\n             Recognizing that the information and data gaps identified in the global report do not\n       necessarily represent gaps in the implementation of the Framework or a lack of commitment to the\n       Framework,\n              Stressing that many Parties face institutional, technical, capacity and resource challenges in\n       preparing national biodiversity strategies and action plans and national reports, in particular in\n       relation to the use of the headline indicators in the monitoring framework for the Kunming-Montreal\n       Global Biodiversity Framework,3 which contribute to the information gaps in the global report,\n              Acknowledging that, while there were methodological, scientific, technical and data limitations\n       and time constraints related to the preparation of the first global report, the global report is the most\n       definitive source of information on collective progress currently available, that it was developed\n       through a Party-led process and that it provides a useful basis for the global review,\n             Acknowledging also that the planning, monitoring, reporting and review mechanisms,\n       including future global reviews, could be further strengthened by taking into account the experiences\n       and lessons learned in preparing the first global report and the seventh national reports,\n              Expressing appreciation for the work of the Ad Hoc Scientific and Technical Advisory Group\n       for the Preparation of the Global Report on Collective Progress in the Implementation of the\n       Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework in guiding the production of the first global\n       report,\n              Acknowledging with appreciation the financial support provided by the Governments of\n       Germany and Netherlands (Kingdom of the) and, through the China Trust Fund, the United Nations\n       Environment Programme for the global review, as well as the financial support provided by the\n       European Union and the Governments of Netherlands (Kingdom of the) and Switzerland and,\n       through the China Trust Fund, the United Nations Environment Programme for the preparation of\n       the related global report,\n              Expressing its appreciation to the Government of China for hosting the Kunming dialogue on\n       collective progress in the implementation of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework\n       in support of the global review, with Canada and China serving as Co-Chairs,\n             1.    Welcomes with appreciation the global report on collective progress in the\n       implementation of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, 4 and notes its\n       overarching messages and key messages;\n             2.    Also welcomes with appreciation the fact that the Framework has generated\n       unprecedented global engagement and has driven action, innovation, collaboration and learning;\n\n2\n  The timing of disbursement and expenditure for the enabling activities projects funded by the Global Environment Facility is\navailable in document CBD/SBI/7/INF/17 and summarized in the global report.\n3 Decision 15/5, annex I.\n4 CBD/COP/17/INF/7.\n\n\n\n2/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18</p>"},{"md":"**關切地注意到**，國家報告中仍存在資料與資訊缺口，且並非所有締約方都能及時提交國家報告，以供全球報告編製時進行審議，因此，部分締約方（特別是小島嶼開發中國家）的經驗未能充分反映在全球報告中，","html":"<p><strong>關切地注意到</strong>，國家報告中仍存在資料與資訊缺口，且並非所有締約方都能及時提交國家報告，以供全球報告編製時進行審議，因此，部分締約方（特別是小島嶼開發中國家）的經驗未能充分反映在全球報告中，</p>"},{"md":"**注意到**並表示感謝，各締約方在制定國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告方面獲得了支持，","html":"<p><strong>注意到</strong>並表示感謝，各締約方在制定國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告方面獲得了支持，</p>"},{"md":"**強調**對於許多開發中國家締約方，特別是小島嶼開發中國家，全球環境基金在支持制定國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告方面的啟動仍持續存在延遲，這影響了部分國家報告的及時性與完整性，² 並**承認**一系列因素可能導致了這些延遲，","html":"<p><strong>強調</strong>對於許多開發中國家締約方，特別是小島嶼開發中國家，全球環境基金在支持制定國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告方面的啟動仍持續存在延遲，這影響了部分國家報告的及時性與完整性，² 並<strong>承認</strong>一系列因素可能導致了這些延遲，</p>"},{"md":"**承認**在全球報告中所識別出的資訊與資料缺口，並不一定代表綱要執行方面的缺口或對綱要缺乏承諾，","html":"<p><strong>承認</strong>在全球報告中所識別出的資訊與資料缺口，並不一定代表綱要執行方面的缺口或對綱要缺乏承諾，</p>"},{"md":"**強調**許多締約方在編製國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告時，面臨制度、技術、能力與資源方面的挑戰，特別是關於在昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要監測架構中使用主要指標方面，³ 這導致了全球報告中的資訊缺口，","html":"<p><strong>強調</strong>許多締約方在編製國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告時，面臨制度、技術、能力與資源方面的挑戰，特別是關於在昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要監測架構中使用主要指標方面，³ 這導致了全球報告中的資訊缺口，</p>"},{"md":"**承認**雖然在編製第一份全球報告時存在方法學、科學、技術與資料的限制以及時間壓力，但全球報告是目前可用於說明集體進展最權威的資訊來源，且該報告是透過締約方主導的程序所開發，並為全球審查提供了有用的基礎，","html":"<p><strong>承認</strong>雖然在編製第一份全球報告時存在方法學、科學、技術與資料的限制以及時間壓力，但全球報告是目前可用於說明集體進展最權威的資訊來源，且該報告是透過締約方主導的程序所開發，並為全球審查提供了有用的基礎，</p>"},{"md":"**同時承認**規劃、監測、報告與審查機制（包括未來的全球審查）可以透過考慮編製第一份全球報告與第七次國家報告的經驗與教訓，而得到進一步強化，","html":"<p><strong>同時承認</strong>規劃、監測、報告與審查機制（包括未來的全球審查）可以透過考慮編製第一份全球報告與第七次國家報告的經驗與教訓，而得到進一步強化，</p>"},{"md":"**表示感謝**「為編製昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行集體進展全球報告之特設科學與技術諮詢專家小組」在指導第一份全球報告製作方面所做的努力，","html":"<p><strong>表示感謝</strong>「為編製昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行集體進展全球報告之特設科學與技術諮詢專家小組」在指導第一份全球報告製作方面所做的努力，</p>"},{"md":"**表示感謝並承認**德國政府、荷蘭政府（荷蘭王國）以及透過中國信託基金（China Trust Fund）的聯合國環境規劃署為全球審查提供的財務支持，以及歐洲聯盟、荷蘭政府（荷蘭王國）、瑞士政府及透過中國信託基金的聯合國環境規劃署為編製相關全球報告所提供的財務支持，","html":"<p><strong>表示感謝並承認</strong>德國政府、荷蘭政府（荷蘭王國）以及透過中國信託基金（China Trust Fund）的聯合國環境規劃署為全球審查提供的財務支持，以及歐洲聯盟、荷蘭政府（荷蘭王國）、瑞士政府及透過中國信託基金的聯合國環境規劃署為編製相關全球報告所提供的財務支持，</p>"},{"md":"**表示感謝**中國政府在加拿大與中國擔任共同主席的情況下，主辦了支持全球審查之「關於昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行集體進展之昆明對話」，","html":"<p><strong>表示感謝</strong>中國政府在加拿大與中國擔任共同主席的情況下，主辦了支持全球審查之「關於昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行集體進展之昆明對話」，</p>"},{"md":"1.\n**表示感謝並歡迎**關於昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行集體進展之全球報告，⁴ 並注意到其核心訊息與關鍵訊息；","html":"<p>1.<br />\n<strong>表示感謝並歡迎</strong>關於昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要執行集體進展之全球報告，⁴ 並注意到其核心訊息與關鍵訊息；</p>"},{"md":"2.\n**亦表示感謝並歡迎**綱要已產生前所未有的全球參與，並驅動了行動、創新、協作與學習；","html":"<p>2.<br />\n<strong>亦表示感謝並歡迎</strong>綱要已產生前所未有的全球參與，並驅動了行動、創新、協作與學習；</p>"},{"md":"---\n² 由全球環境基金資助之啟動活動專案，其撥款與支出的時間點詳見文件 CBD/SBI/7/INF/17，並彙整於全球報告中。\n³ 決議 15/5，附件 I。\n⁴ CBD/COP/17/INF/7。","html":"<hr />\n<p>² 由全球環境基金資助之啟動活動專案，其撥款與支出的時間點詳見文件 CBD/SBI/7/INF/17，並彙整於全球報告中。<br />\n³ 決議 15/5，附件 I。<br />\n⁴ CBD/COP/17/INF/7。</p>"}]},{"p":"p003","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p003.md","en":"                                                                                                  CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n            3.    Welcomes the fact that Parties have taken actions across all 23 targets of the Framework\n       showing successful examples of implementation for every target;\n             4.     Also welcomes the fact that actors other than national Governments, namely, indigenous\n       peoples and local communities, subnational governments, cities, and other local authorities,\n       intergovernmental organizations, other multilateral environment agreements, non-governmental\n       organizations, women, youth, research organizations, the business and finance community and\n       representatives of sectors related to or dependent on biodiversity have taken action to implement the\n       Framework;5\n             5.    Highlights the fact that, as indicated in the global report, the review has revealed both\n       progress and gaps in implementation, in particular that:\n               (a)     Scope and coverage:\n                       (i)     Parties have collectively set national targets that had a higher level of scope and\n                               coverage of Targets 4, 5, 8, 9, 10 and 11 of the Framework, an intermediate level\n                               of scope and coverage of Targets 2, 3, 6, 7, 12, 13, 17 and 21 and a lower level\n                               of scope and coverage of Targets 1, 14, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20, 22 and 23;\n                       (ii)    The consideration of marine, coastal and inland water ecosystems, issues related\n                               to the drivers of biodiversity loss and issues related to section C of the Framework\n                               has been less addressed in national biodiversity strategies and action plans,\n                               national targets and national reports;\n               (b)     Action and implementation:\n                       (i)     National targets associated with Targets 1, 2, 3, 8, 13, 20, 21 and 22 of the\n                               Framework were most frequently self-assessed by Parties as being on track, while\n                               national targets associated with Targets 6, 12, 15, 16, 18 and 19 were least\n                               frequently self-assessed by Parties as being on track; however, less than half of\n                               the national targets associated with any global target had been reported by Parties\n                               as being on track;\n                       (ii)    While there were data and national monitoring capacity gaps that limited the\n                               ability to report national data and, thus, to aggregate them at the global level and\n                               conclusively track collective progress towards the global targets, indicators\n                               related to the targets of the Framework showed negative trends more commonly\n                               than positive ones;\n               (c)     Means of implementation:\n                       (i)     Means of implementation remain below what is required for the full and effective\n                               implementation of the Framework, especially for developing country Parties, in\n                               particular the least developed countries and the small island developing States;\n                       (ii)    Since the adoption of the Framework, there has been some, albeit uneven,\n                               progress across Parties in domestic and international public finance, positive\n                               incentives for biodiversity, capacity-building and development, access to and\n                               transfer of technology and technical and scientific cooperation;\n                       (iii)   Collectively, limited progress has been made in mobilizing and tracking private\n                               sector finance and positive incentives and in reducing incentives, including\n                               subsidies, harmful to biodiversity;\n                       (iv)    The lack of availability of sufficient and timely means of implementation was\n                               identified as the most common barrier to implementation, especially by\n\n5 All references to “actors other than national Governments” are to be understood as described in paragraph 4 (in line with\n\ndecisions 15/6 and 16/32).\n\n                                                                                                                              3/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18</p>"},{"md":"3.\n**歡迎**締約方已針對《綱要》全部 23 項目標採取行動，並為每一項目標展示了成功的執行案例；","html":"<p>3.<br />\n<strong>歡迎</strong>締約方已針對《綱要》全部 23 項目標採取行動，並為每一項目標展示了成功的執行案例；</p>"},{"md":"4.\n亦**歡迎**國家政府以外的行動者，即原住民族與地方社區、次國家級政府、城市及其他地方當局、政府間組織、其他多邊環境協議、非政府組織、女性、青年、研究機構、企業與金融界，以及與生物多樣性相關或依賴生物多樣性的部門代表，已採取行動來執行《綱要》；⁵","html":"<p>4.<br />\n亦<strong>歡迎</strong>國家政府以外的行動者，即原住民族與地方社區、次國家級政府、城市及其他地方當局、政府間組織、其他多邊環境協議、非政府組織、女性、青年、研究機構、企業與金融界，以及與生物多樣性相關或依賴生物多樣性的部門代表，已採取行動來執行《綱要》；⁵</p>"},{"md":"5.\n**強調**如全球報告所述，審查揭示了執行方面的進展與差距，特別是：\n(a) 範圍與覆蓋範圍：\n(i) 締約方集體設定的國家目標，對於《綱要》目標 4、5、8、9、10 及 11 具有較高程度的範圍與覆蓋範圍；對於目標 2、3、6、7、12、13、17 及 21 具有中等程度的範圍與覆蓋範圍；對於目標 1、14、15、16、18、19、20、22 及 23 則具有較低程度的範圍與覆蓋範圍；\n(ii) 海洋、沿岸及內陸水域生態系、與生物多樣性流失驅動因素相關的問題，以及與《綱要》C 節相關的問題，在國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及國家報告中較少被處理；","html":"<p>5.<br />\n<strong>強調</strong>如全球報告所述，審查揭示了執行方面的進展與差距，特別是：<br />\n(a) 範圍與覆蓋範圍：<br />\n(i) 締約方集體設定的國家目標，對於《綱要》目標 4、5、8、9、10 及 11 具有較高程度的範圍與覆蓋範圍；對於目標 2、3、6、7、12、13、17 及 21 具有中等程度的範圍與覆蓋範圍；對於目標 1、14、15、16、18、19、20、22 及 23 則具有較低程度的範圍與覆蓋範圍；<br />\n(ii) 海洋、沿岸及內陸水域生態系、與生物多樣性流失驅動因素相關的問題，以及與《綱要》C 節相關的問題，在國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及國家報告中較少被處理；</p>"},{"md":"(b) 行動與執行：\n(i) 締約方自我評估認為與《綱要》目標 1、2、3、8、13、20、21 及 22 相關的國家目標最常處於軌道上，而與目標 6、12、15、16、18 及 19 相關的國家目標，其自我評估為處於軌道上的頻率最低；然而，與任何全球目標相關的國家目標中，由締約方報告為處於軌道上的比例不足一半；\n(ii) 雖然存在資料與國家監測能力的差距，限制了報告國家資料並進而於全球層級進行彙總以及結論性地追蹤全球目標集體進展的能力，但與《綱要》目標相關的指標顯示負面趨勢的頻率高於正面趨勢；","html":"<p>(b) 行動與執行：<br />\n(i) 締約方自我評估認為與《綱要》目標 1、2、3、8、13、20、21 及 22 相關的國家目標最常處於軌道上，而與目標 6、12、15、16、18 及 19 相關的國家目標，其自我評估為處於軌道上的頻率最低；然而，與任何全球目標相關的國家目標中，由締約方報告為處於軌道上的比例不足一半；<br />\n(ii) 雖然存在資料與國家監測能力的差距，限制了報告國家資料並進而於全球層級進行彙總以及結論性地追蹤全球目標集體進展的能力，但與《綱要》目標相關的指標顯示負面趨勢的頻率高於正面趨勢；</p>"},{"md":"(c) 執行手段：\n(i) 執行手段仍低於全面且有效執行《綱要》之需求，對於開發中國家締約方，特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家而言更是如此；\n(ii) 自《綱要》通過以來，各締約方在國內與國際公共財政、生物多樣性正面誘因、能力建構與發展、技術取得與轉讓，以及技術與科學合作方面，雖有進展但並不均衡；\n(iii) 集體而言，在動員與追蹤私部門資金與正面誘因，以及減少包括補貼在內對生物多樣性有害的誘因方面，進展有限；\n(iv) 缺乏充足且及時的執行手段被認為是執行中最常見的障礙，特別是對於","html":"<p>(c) 執行手段：<br />\n(i) 執行手段仍低於全面且有效執行《綱要》之需求，對於開發中國家締約方，特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家而言更是如此；<br />\n(ii) 自《綱要》通過以來，各締約方在國內與國際公共財政、生物多樣性正面誘因、能力建構與發展、技術取得與轉讓，以及技術與科學合作方面，雖有進展但並不均衡；<br />\n(iii) 集體而言，在動員與追蹤私部門資金與正面誘因，以及減少包括補貼在內對生物多樣性有害的誘因方面，進展有限；<br />\n(iv) 缺乏充足且及時的執行手段被認為是執行中最常見的障礙，特別是對於</p>"},{"md":"---\n⁵ 所有提及「國家政府以外的行動者」之處，均應理解為如第 4 段所述（與決議 15/6 及 16/32 一致）。","html":"<hr />\n<p>⁵ 所有提及「國家政府以外的行動者」之處，均應理解為如第 4 段所述（與決議 15/6 及 16/32 一致）。</p>"}]},{"p":"p004","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p004.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n                        developing country Parties, in particular the least developed countries and the\n                        small island developing States;\n          (d)   Whole-of-government approaches:\n                (i)     Parties have made progress in strengthening environmental governance,\n                        including by engaging all levels of government and all sectors; however, gaps\n                        remain with regard to policy coherence, mainstreaming biodiversity and its\n                        multiple values into national policy and engaging ministries beyond the\n                        environmental sector in implementation;\n                (ii)    Gaps remain with regard to progress towards Targets 1 and 14 of the Framework,\n                        which relate to integrated planning and mainstreaming and are most directly\n                        related to whole-of-government approaches, especially in relation to integrated\n                        spatial planning, integrating biodiversity and its multiple values into policies,\n                        regulations, planning and development processes and ensuring integration across\n                        all levels of government and sectors;\n          (e)   Whole-of-society approaches:\n                (i)     Actors other than national Governments are actively supporting the\n                        implementation of the Framework, but indigenous peoples and local\n                        communities, women, youth and persons with disabilities face challenges related\n                        to limited resources, technical capacities, access to information and knowledge,\n                        effective participation and coordination mechanisms;\n                (ii)    Collectively, Parties have made progress in engaging actors other than national\n                        Governments in planning and implementation; however, many Parties reported\n                        challenges in accounting for the contributions of those actors in national\n                        implementation efforts;\n                (iii)   While many Parties reported progress towards some elements of Targets 22 and\n                        23 of the Framework, such as involving to a varying degree indigenous peoples\n                        and local communities, women and youth, persons with disabilities and other\n                        actors defined in those targets in biodiversity-related decision-making, issues\n                        related to the following were less well reflected in national targets: access to\n                        information and justice, rights over lands, territories, resources and traditional\n                        knowledge, and the protection of environmental human rights defenders or\n                        gender-responsive biodiversity governance;\n          (f)   Data and knowledge:\n                (i)     Parties have demonstrated improvements to national monitoring and knowledge\n                        management systems, including accounting for traditional knowledge and\n                        collecting and using information from government agencies, scientific\n                        institutions, indigenous peoples and local communities, civil society and\n                        international sources, as reflected in the progress made towards Target 21 of the\n                        Framework;\n                (ii)    A total of 116 Parties reported on at least one headline indicator; the headline\n                        indicators on protected area extent (3.1), Red List Index (A.3), international\n                        public funding (D.1) and domestic public funding (D.2), which are associated\n                        with Targets 3, 4 and 19 of the Framework, were the headline indicators most\n                        reported on, while the indicators related to land-use change and land tenure in the\n                        traditional territories of indigenous peoples and local communities (22.1),\n                        benefits from the sustainable use of wild species (9.1), index of coastal\n                        eutrophication (7.1) and monetary benefits received in accordance with\n                        applicable internationally agreed access and benefit-sharing instruments (C.1),\n\n4/11","blocks":[{"md":"開發中國家締約方，特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家；","html":"<p>開發中國家締約方，特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家；</p>"},{"md":"(d) 全政府方法：\n(i) 締約方在強化環境治理方面已取得進展，包括參與各級政府與所有部門；然而，在政策一致性、將生物多樣性及其多重價值主流化至國家政策，以及在執行過程中參與環境部門以外的部會方面，仍存在差距；","html":"<p>(d) 全政府方法：<br />\n(i) 締約方在強化環境治理方面已取得進展，包括參與各級政府與所有部門；然而，在政策一致性、將生物多樣性及其多重價值主流化至國家政策，以及在執行過程中參與環境部門以外的部會方面，仍存在差距；</p>"},{"md":"(ii) 在達成《綱要》目標 1 與目標 14 的進展方面仍存在差距，該等目標涉及整合規劃與主流化，且與全政府方法最直接相關，特別是在整合性空間規劃、將生物多樣性及其多重價值整合至政策、法規、規劃與發展程序，以及確保跨所有政府層級與部門的整合方面；","html":"<p>(ii) 在達成《綱要》目標 1 與目標 14 的進展方面仍存在差距，該等目標涉及整合規劃與主流化，且與全政府方法最直接相關，特別是在整合性空間規劃、將生物多樣性及其多重價值整合至政策、法規、規劃與發展程序，以及確保跨所有政府層級與部門的整合方面；</p>"},{"md":"(e) 全社會方法：\n(i) 除了國家政府以外的行動者正積極支持《綱要》的執行，但原住民族與地方社區、女性、青年及身心障礙者面臨與資源有限、技術能力、取得資訊與知識、有效參與及協調機制相關的挑戰；","html":"<p>(e) 全社會方法：<br />\n(i) 除了國家政府以外的行動者正積極支持《綱要》的執行，但原住民族與地方社區、女性、青年及身心障礙者面臨與資源有限、技術能力、取得資訊與知識、有效參與及協調機制相關的挑戰；</p>"},{"md":"(ii) 總體而言，締約方在參與國家政府以外的行動者參與規劃與執行方面已取得進展；然而，許多締約方報告在計算該等行動者於國家執行工作中的貢獻時面臨挑戰；","html":"<p>(ii) 總體而言，締約方在參與國家政府以外的行動者參與規劃與執行方面已取得進展；然而，許多締約方報告在計算該等行動者於國家執行工作中的貢獻時面臨挑戰；</p>"},{"md":"(iii) 雖然許多締約方報告在達成《綱要》目標 22 與目標 23 的某些要素方面取得進展，例如在程度不一的情況下，讓原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年、身心障礙者及該等目標中所定義的其他行動者參與生物多樣性相關決策，但與下列事項相關的問題在國家目標中反映較少：取得資訊與司法、對土地、領土、資源與傳統知識的權利，以及環境人權捍衛者的保護或具性別回應性的生物多樣性治理；","html":"<p>(iii) 雖然許多締約方報告在達成《綱要》目標 22 與目標 23 的某些要素方面取得進展，例如在程度不一的情況下，讓原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年、身心障礙者及該等目標中所定義的其他行動者參與生物多樣性相關決策，但與下列事項相關的問題在國家目標中反映較少：取得資訊與司法、對土地、領土、資源與傳統知識的權利，以及環境人權捍衛者的保護或具性別回應性的生物多樣性治理；</p>"},{"md":"(f) 資料與知識：\n(i) 締約方已展現出國家監測與知識管理系統的改善，包括納入傳統知識的計算，以及收集並使用來自政府機構、科學機構、原住民族與地方社區、公民社會及國際來源的資訊，這反映在達成《綱要》目標 21 的進展中；","html":"<p>(f) 資料與知識：<br />\n(i) 締約方已展現出國家監測與知識管理系統的改善，包括納入傳統知識的計算，以及收集並使用來自政府機構、科學機構、原住民族與地方社區、公民社會及國際來源的資訊，這反映在達成《綱要》目標 21 的進展中；</p>"},{"md":"(ii) 共有 116 個締約方報告了至少一項主要指標；與《綱要》目標 3、4 與 19 相關的保護區範圍（3.1）、紅色名錄指數（A.3）、國際公共資金（D.1）及國內公共資金（D.2）的主要指標，是報告數量最多的主要指標；而與原住民族與地方社區傳統領土內的土地利用變化與土地權屬（22.1）、野生物種永續利用之效益（9.1）、海岸優養化指數（7.1）以及根據適用的國際公認取得與利益分享工具所獲得之金錢效益（C.1）相關的指標，","html":"<p>(ii) 共有 116 個締約方報告了至少一項主要指標；與《綱要》目標 3、4 與 19 相關的保護區範圍（3.1）、紅色名錄指數（A.3）、國際公共資金（D.1）及國內公共資金（D.2）的主要指標，是報告數量最多的主要指標；而與原住民族與地方社區傳統領土內的土地利用變化與土地權屬（22.1）、野生物種永續利用之效益（9.1）、海岸優養化指數（7.1）以及根據適用的國際公認取得與利益分享工具所獲得之金錢效益（C.1）相關的指標，</p>"}]},{"p":"p005","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p005.md","en":"                                                                                        CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n                                 which are associated with Targets 7, 9 and 13, were the indicators least reported\n                                 on;\n                        (iii)    For 19 of the 23 targets of the Framework, less than half of the reporting Parties\n                                 submitted any data for a headline, component, complementary or national\n                                 indicator; the targets with the most reported quantitative data were 1, 2, 3, 4\n                                 and 10, while Targets 8, 14, 16, 17, 20, 22 and 23 received the least;\n                        (iv)     The use of disaggregated indicator information, including by ecosystem type, sex,\n                                 age, indigenous people and local community status or other relevant\n                                 characteristics, was limited;\n                        (v)      Reporting against the indicators and monitoring efforts have been negatively\n                                 affected by gaps, challenges and barriers related to methodologies, data\n                                 availability and access, data fragmentation, technical and technological\n                                 infrastructure, resource availability and capacity;\n               6.     Notes with deep concern that, unless collective implementation is rapidly accelerated,\n        including through enhanced international cooperation, improved actions and efforts and strengthened\n        means of implementation, the 2030 targets and mission of the Framework will not be achieved, and\n        stresses the imperative to address gaps, challenges and barriers in implementation to achieve the 2030\n        targets and mission;\n              7.    Also notes with deep concern that, if the 2030 targets and mission of the Framework are\n        not achieved, its 2050 goals and vision may be at risk, especially if action and the provision and\n        mobilization of means of implementation are not rapidly accelerated;\n              8.     Recalls that the goals and targets of the Framework are global in nature and that each\n        Party would contribute to attaining the goals and targets of the Framework in accordance with\n        national circumstances, priorities and capabilities, as well as the availability of means of\n        implementation;\n               9.    Agrees that the provisions of the present decision should be used to guide and prioritize\n        future work over the period 2026–2030, in order to address gaps, challenges and barriers and foster\n        possible opportunities to enhance collective progress in implementation and means of\n        implementation in relation to the 2030 targets and mission, for all Parties, but especially for\n        developing country Parties, in particular least developed countries and the small island developing\n        States, and that the provisions will be interpreted as being facilitative, non-intrusive and non-\n        punitive;\n              10. Notes that the present decision is complemented by relevant decisions adopted by the\n        Conference of the Parties at its seventeenth meeting and that the implementation of those decisions\n        should be informed, as appropriate, by the present decision;\n               11. Also notes that the implementation of the Framework is supported by relevant decisions\n        of the Conference of the Parties serving as the meetings of the Parties to the Cartagena Protocol on\n        Biosafety6 and the Nagoya Protocol on Access to Genetic Resources and the Fair and Equitable\n        Sharing of Benefits Arising from Their Utilization,7 and recognizes that the governing bodies of the\n        respective Protocols may take into account, as appropriate, relevant provisions of the present\n        decision;\n              12. Encourages Parties, and invites indigenous peoples and local communities, subnational\n        governments, cities and other local authorities, intergovernmental organizations, other multilateral\n        environment agreements, non-governmental organizations, women’s and youth organizations,\n        research organizations, the business and finance community and representatives of sectors related to\n\n6 United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 2226, No. 30619.\n7 Ibid., vol. 3008, No. 30619.\n\n\n\n                                                                                                              5/11","blocks":[{"md":"與目標 7、9 及 13 相關之指標，其報告率最低；","html":"<p>與目標 7、9 及 13 相關之指標，其報告率最低；</p>"},{"md":"(iii)\n在綱要的 23 個目標中，有 19 個目標的報告締約方中，少於半數針對主要指標、組成指標、補充指標或國家指標提交任何資料；報告量最多的定量資料目標為 1、2、3、4 及 10，而目標 8、14、16、17、20、22 及 23 獲得的報告量最少；","html":"<p>(iii)<br />\n在綱要的 23 個目標中，有 19 個目標的報告締約方中，少於半數針對主要指標、組成指標、補充指標或國家指標提交任何資料；報告量最多的定量資料目標為 1、2、3、4 及 10，而目標 8、14、16、17、20、22 及 23 獲得的報告量最少；</p>"},{"md":"(v)\n針對指標的報告與監測工作，受到與方法論、資料可用性與取得、資料碎片化、技術與工藝基礎設施、資源可用性及能力相關之差距、挑戰與障礙的負面影響；","html":"<p>(v)<br />\n針對指標的報告與監測工作，受到與方法論、資料可用性與取得、資料碎片化、技術與工藝基礎設施、資源可用性及能力相關之差距、挑戰與障礙的負面影響；</p>"},{"md":"(iv)\n分類指標資訊的使用有限，包括依據生態系類型、性別、年齡、原住民族與地方社區地位或其他相關特徵進行分類；","html":"<p>(iv)<br />\n分類指標資訊的使用有限，包括依據生態系類型、性別、年齡、原住民族與地方社區地位或其他相關特徵進行分類；</p>"},{"md":"針對方法論、資料可用性與取得、資料碎片化、技術與工藝基礎設施、資源可用性及能力相關之差距、挑戰與障礙，進行指標報告與監測工作的努力受到了負面影響；","html":"<p>針對方法論、資料可用性與取得、資料碎片化、技術與工藝基礎設施、資源可用性及能力相關之差距、挑戰與障礙，進行指標報告與監測工作的努力受到了負面影響；</p>"},{"md":"6.\n**深切關切地注意到**，除非透過加強國際合作、改善行動與努力以及強化執行手段，迅速加速集體執行，否則綱要的 2030 年目標與使命將無法達成，並**強調**必須解決執行過程中的差距、挑戰與障礙，以實現 2030 年目標與使命；","html":"<p>6.<br />\n<strong>深切關切地注意到</strong>，除非透過加強國際合作、改善行動與努力以及強化執行手段，迅速加速集體執行，否則綱要的 2030 年目標與使命將無法達成，並<strong>強調</strong>必須解決執行過程中的差距、挑戰與障礙，以實現 2030 年目標與使命；</p>"},{"md":"7.\n亦**深切關切地注意到**，若綱要的 2030 年目標與使命未能達成，其 2050 年長期目標與願景可能面臨風險，特別是在行動以及執行手段的提供與動員未能迅速加速的情況下；","html":"<p>7.<br />\n亦<strong>深切關切地注意到</strong>，若綱要的 2030 年目標與使命未能達成，其 2050 年長期目標與願景可能面臨風險，特別是在行動以及執行手段的提供與動員未能迅速加速的情況下；</p>"},{"md":"8.\n**回顧**綱要的長期目標與目標具有全球性質，且各締約方應根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，以及執行手段的可用性，為達成綱要的長期目標與目標做出貢獻；","html":"<p>8.<br />\n<strong>回顧</strong>綱要的長期目標與目標具有全球性質，且各締約方應根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，以及執行手段的可用性，為達成綱要的長期目標與目標做出貢獻；</p>"},{"md":"9.\n**同意**本決議之規定應用於指導並優先安排 2026–2030 年期間的未來工作，以解決差距、挑戰與障礙，並促進可能之機會，藉此增進所有締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）在與 2030 年目標及使命相關之執行與執行手段方面的集體進展，且該等規定應被解釋為具促進性、非侵入性且非懲罰性；","html":"<p>9.<br />\n<strong>同意</strong>本決議之規定應用於指導並優先安排 2026–2030 年期間的未來工作，以解決差距、挑戰與障礙，並促進可能之機會，藉此增進所有締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）在與 2030 年目標及使命相關之執行與執行手段方面的集體進展，且該等規定應被解釋為具促進性、非侵入性且非懲罰性；</p>"},{"md":"10. **注意到**本決議與締約方大會於其第十七次會議所採納之相關決議互為補充，且在適當情況下，該等決議之執行應參考本決議；","html":"<p>10. <strong>注意到</strong>本決議與締約方大會於其第十七次會議所採納之相關決議互為補充，且在適當情況下，該等決議之執行應參考本決議；</p>"},{"md":"11. 亦**注意到**綱要之執行受到卡塔赫納生物安全議定書$^6$與名古屋議定書$^7$之締約方會議（即兩議定書之締約方會議）相關決議之支持，並**認可**各議定書之管理機構在適當情況下，得考慮本決議之相關規定；","html":"<p>11. 亦<strong>注意到</strong>綱要之執行受到卡塔赫納生物安全議定書<sup>6</sup>與名古屋議定書<sup>7</sup>之締約方會議（即兩議定書之締約方會議）相關決議之支持，並<strong>認可</strong>各議定書之管理機構在適當情況下，得考慮本決議之相關規定；</p>"},{"md":"12. **鼓勵**締約方，並**邀請**原住民族與地方社區、次國家級政府、城市與其他地方當局、政府間組織、其他多邊環境協定、非政府組織、女性與青年組織、研究機構、工商界與金融界，以及與生物多樣性相關","html":"<p>12. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，並<strong>邀請</strong>原住民族與地方社區、次國家級政府、城市與其他地方當局、政府間組織、其他多邊環境協定、非政府組織、女性與青年組織、研究機構、工商界與金融界，以及與生物多樣性相關</p>"},{"md":"---\n$^6$ United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 2226, No. 30619.\n$^7$ Ibid., vol. 3008, No. 30619.","html":"<hr />\n<p><sup>6</sup> United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 2226, No. 30619.<br />\n<sup>7</sup> Ibid., vol. 3008, No. 30619.</p>"}]},{"p":"p006","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p006.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n       or dependent on biodiversity, to consider, as appropriate and relevant, the non-prescriptive\n       approaches 8 identified in the global report to address the gaps, challenges and barriers in the\n       implementation of the Framework and to foster possible opportunities to enhanced collective\n       progress in implementation, and notes that Parties may also use the policy-relevant actions described\n       in relevant assessments of the Intergovernmental Science-Policy Platform on Biodiversity and\n       Ecosystem Services in that regard;\n              13. Welcomes the organization of the Kunming dialogue, takes note with appreciation of its\n       Co-Chairs’ summary9 and of the report of the dialogue,10 while recognizing that the dialogue was\n       held in a non-negotiating setting, and notes that Parties, other Governments and relevant actors may\n       wish to consider, as appropriate and relevant, the messages, approaches and lessons identified in the\n       dialogue in their efforts to accelerate the implementation of the Framework and to address the gaps,\n       challenges and barriers identified in the global review;\n       Yerevan pathway for accelerating the implementation\n       of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework\n             14. Reaffirms the Parties’ commitment to the full and effective implementation of the\n       Framework, including by taking into account the considerations in its section C, in accordance with\n       national circumstances, priorities and capabilities, to advance the three objectives of the Convention\n       in a balanced and mutually supportive manner, including through enhanced international\n       cooperation, improved actions and efforts and strengthened means of implementation;\n       Accelerating action and implementation\n             15. Urges Parties to accelerate the implementation of the Framework, including by\n       increased ambition in addressing the gaps, challenges and barriers related to implementation,\n       recognizing that enhanced support for developing country Parties, in particular the least developed\n       countries and the small island developing States, will allow for higher ambition in their actions;\n              [16 Alt.1    Emphasizes that accelerating implementation would need to be supported through\n       increased action to address the underlying drivers of biodiversity loss and global transformation of\n       economic and financial systems [, improving the practices of economic sectors that have a negative\n       impact on nature][, as well as the alignment of financial flows with the [Framework][biodiversity\n       objectives]][and barriers and dis-enablers to implementation, such as unilateral and coercive trade\n       measures, which act as impediments to socioeconomic, economic and sustainable development in\n       developing countries];]\n             [16. Alt.2 Emphasizes that accelerating implementation would need to be supported through\n       increased action to address the underlying drivers of biodiversity loss;]\n             [16. (proposal)     Emphasizes that accelerating implementation would need to be supported\n       through increased action to address the underlying drivers of biodiversity loss;]\n             17. Invites actors other than national Governments to accelerate their contributions to the\n       implementation of the Framework and support the implementation priorities and needs of Parties, as\n       appropriate;\n              18. Welcomes the voluntary coalitions, partnerships, initiatives and multi-stakeholder\n       platforms that have been established to support the implementation of the Framework, and\n       encourages Parties, and invites other Governments and actors other than national Governments, to\n       make effective use of relevant existing initiatives, and, where needed, establish new ones, aimed at\n       enhancing the implementation of the Framework, prioritizing the targets and cross-cutting elements\n       of the Framework for which Parties have reported the least progress towards their national targets,\n       as identified in subparagraph 5 (b) above, and in a manner that is supportive of implementation by\n\n8 The non-prescriptive approaches are listed in section III of the global report (CBD/COP/17/INF/7).\n9 CBD/IMP/OM/2026/1/8.\n10 CBD/IMP/OM/2026/1/9.\n\n\n\n6/11","blocks":[{"md":"或依賴生物多樣性之部門代表，並視適當且相關之情況，考慮全球報告中所識別之非指令性方法 8，以應對綱要執行過程中的差距、挑戰與障礙，並促進可能增進執行進度之集體進展之機會；並注意到締約方在此方面亦可使用政府間科學－政策平台關於生物多樣性及生態系服務之相關評估中所描述之具政策相關性之行動；","html":"<p>或依賴生物多樣性之部門代表，並視適當且相關之情況，考慮全球報告中所識別之非指令性方法 8，以應對綱要執行過程中的差距、挑戰與障礙，並促進可能增進執行進度之集體進展之機會；並注意到締約方在此方面亦可使用政府間科學－政策平台關於生物多樣性及生態系服務之相關評估中所描述之具政策相關性之行動；</p>"},{"md":"13. **歡迎**昆明對話之舉行，並**注意到**並感謝其共同主席之摘要 9 及對話報告 10，同時承認該對話是在非談判性質之環境下舉行，並注意到締約方、其他政府及相關行動者在致力於加速綱要執行，以及應對全球審查中所識別之差距、挑戰與障礙時，得視適當且相關之情況，考慮對話中所識別之訊息、方法與經驗；","html":"<p>13. <strong>歡迎</strong>昆明對話之舉行，並<strong>注意到</strong>並感謝其共同主席之摘要 9 及對話報告 10，同時承認該對話是在非談判性質之環境下舉行，並注意到締約方、其他政府及相關行動者在致力於加速綱要執行，以及應對全球審查中所識別之差距、挑戰與障礙時，得視適當且相關之情況，考慮對話中所識別之訊息、方法與經驗；</p>"},{"md":"**加速執行《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》之葉里溫路徑**","html":"<p><strong>加速執行《昆明－蒙特婁全球生物多樣性綱要》之葉里溫路徑</strong></p>"},{"md":"14. **重申**締約方致力於全面且有效執行綱要之承諾，包括根據各國國情、優先事項與能力，並考量綱要 C 節之考量因素，以平衡且相互支持之方式推進公約之三大目標，包括透過加強國際合作、改善行動與努力，以及強化執行手段；","html":"<p>14. <strong>重申</strong>締約方致力於全面且有效執行綱要之承諾，包括根據各國國情、優先事項與能力，並考量綱要 C 節之考量因素，以平衡且相互支持之方式推進公約之三大目標，包括透過加強國際合作、改善行動與努力，以及強化執行手段；</p>"},{"md":"**加速行動與執行**","html":"<p><strong>加速行動與執行</strong></p>"},{"md":"15. **敦促**締約方加速綱要之執行，包括透過增加雄心以應對與執行相關之差距、挑戰與障礙，並承認加強對開發中國家締約方，特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家之支持，將有助於提升其行動之雄心；","html":"<p>15. <strong>敦促</strong>締約方加速綱要之執行，包括透過增加雄心以應對與執行相關之差距、挑戰與障礙，並承認加強對開發中國家締約方，特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家之支持，將有助於提升其行動之雄心；</p>"},{"md":"[16. 替案 1\n**強調**加速執行需要透過增加行動來支持，以應對生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素及全球經濟與金融體系之轉型，[, 改善對自然具有負面影響之經濟部門實務][, 以及使金融流向與[綱要][生物多樣性目標]一致][以及執行之障礙與阻礙因素，例如單方面且具強制性之貿易措施，此類措施對開發中國家之社會經濟、經濟與永續發展構成阻礙；];]","html":"<p>[16. 替案 1<br />\n<strong>強調</strong>加速執行需要透過增加行動來支持，以應對生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素及全球經濟與金融體系之轉型，[, 改善對自然具有負面影響之經濟部門實務][, 以及使金融流向與[綱要][生物多樣性目標]一致][以及執行之障礙與阻礙因素，例如單方面且具強制性之貿易措施，此類措施對開發中國家之社會經濟、經濟與永續發展構成阻礙；];]</p>"},{"md":"[16. 替案 2 **強調**加速執行需要透過增加行動來支持，以應對生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素；]","html":"<p>[16. 替案 2 <strong>強調</strong>加速執行需要透過增加行動來支持，以應對生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素；]</p>"},{"md":"[16. (提案)\n**強調**加速執行需要透過增加行動來支持，以應對生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素；]","html":"<p>[16. (提案)<br />\n<strong>強調</strong>加速執行需要透過增加行動來支持，以應對生物多樣性流失之根本驅動因素；]</p>"},{"md":"17. **邀請**國家政府以外之行動者，視適當情況加速其對綱要執行之貢獻，並支持締約方之執行優先事項與需求；","html":"<p>17. <strong>邀請</strong>國家政府以外之行動者，視適當情況加速其對綱要執行之貢獻，並支持締約方之執行優先事項與需求；</p>"},{"md":"18. **歡迎**為支持綱要執行而建立之自願性聯盟、夥伴關係、倡議及多方利害關係人平台，並**鼓勵**締約方，且**邀請**其他政府及國家政府以外之行動者，有效利用相關現有倡議，並在必要時建立新倡議，旨在增進綱要之執行，並優先處理如上述第 5 (b) 小節所識別，締約方對其國家目標之進展最少之綱要目標與橫向要素，且其方式應支持由","html":"<p>18. <strong>歡迎</strong>為支持綱要執行而建立之自願性聯盟、夥伴關係、倡議及多方利害關係人平台，並<strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，且<strong>邀請</strong>其他政府及國家政府以外之行動者，有效利用相關現有倡議，並在必要時建立新倡議，旨在增進綱要之執行，並優先處理如上述第 5 (b) 小節所識別，締約方對其國家目標之進展最少之綱要目標與橫向要素，且其方式應支持由</p>"},{"md":"---\n8 非指令性方法列於全球報告（CBD/COP/17/INF/7）之第三節。\n9 CBD/IMP/OM/2026/1/8。\n10 CBD/IMP/OM/2026/1/9。","html":"<hr />\n<p>8 非指令性方法列於全球報告（CBD/COP/17/INF/7）之第三節。<br />\n9 CBD/IMP/OM/2026/1/8。<br />\n10 CBD/IMP/OM/2026/1/9。</p>"}]},{"p":"p007","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p007.md","en":"                                                                            CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\nParties at the national level, noting that such initiatives may complement and contribute to means of\nimplementation and should not be seen as a substitute for the obligations of all Parties under the\nConvention regarding means of implementation;\n       19. Also welcomes the support provided by relevant partners, including the regional and\nsubregional technical and scientific cooperation support centres, the secretariats of multilateral\nenvironmental agreements, United Nations entities, intergovernmental organizations, non-\ngovernmental organizations and research networks, to implement the Framework, and invites these\npartners to provide further support, paying particular attention to areas where progress remains\ninsufficient, while responding to the needs and priorities identified by Parties, especially developing\ncountry Parties, in particular the least developed countries and the small island development States[,\nand Parties with economies in transition,] including by facilitating peer-to-peer learning, North-\nSouth, South-South and triangular cooperation and information exchange;\n       [19. (proposal)     Also welcomes the support provided by relevant partners, including the\nregional and subregional technical and scientific cooperation support centres, the secretariats of\nmultilateral environmental agreements, United Nations entities, intergovernmental organizations,\nnon-governmental organizations and research networks, to implement the Framework, and invites\nthese partners to provide further support, paying particular attention to areas where progress remains\ninsufficient, while responding to the needs and priorities identified by Parties, especially developing\ncountry Parties, in particular the least developed countries and the small island development States,\nand Parties with economies in transition, including by facilitating peer-to-peer learning, North-South,\nSouth-South and triangular cooperation and information exchange;]\nEnhancing scope and coverage\n      20. Urges Parties that have not yet done so to finalize and submit their revised or updated\nnational biodiversity strategies and action plans, national targets and seventh national report as soon\nas possible, noting that the submission of national biodiversity strategies and action plans and\nnational reports are obligations under Articles 6 and 26 of the Convention, and, where relevant and\nin accordance with national circumstances, priorities and capabilities, take into account the gaps,\nchallenges, barriers and opportunities identified in the global report, in particular the gaps and\nprogress identified in paragraph 5 above;\n      21. Encourages Parties who have not already done so, on a voluntary basis, to develop,\nthrough inclusive processes, national biodiversity finance plans or similar instruments, capacity-\nbuilding and monitoring plans to guide effective implementation, in accordance with national\ncircumstances, priorities and capabilities;\n       22. Recognizes that Parties that wish to do so, and in accordance with national\ncircumstances, priorities and capabilities, may further strengthen their commitments towards the\nimplementation of the Framework through national instruments, as appropriate and where relevant,\nnoting the gaps described in paragraph 5 above, including with regard to marine, coastal and inland\nwater ecosystems, while further recognizing that the extent and pace at which Parties, especially\ndeveloping country Parties, and in particular the least developed countries and the small island\ndeveloping States, are able to do so depend on the availability of adequate, predictable, timely and\neasily accessible means of implementation, and while ensuring that such development or revision\ndoes not divert attention or resources away from actions to implement the Framework;\nStrengthening means of implementation\n       23. Recognizes that the full and effective implementation of the Framework relies, inter alia,\non the availability of sufficient and timely means of implementation, including financial resources,\ncapacity, technology and technical and scientific resources, and that gaps in means of implementation\nare the most common barriers to the full and effective implementation of the Framework, especially\nfor developing country Parties, in particular the least developed countries and the small island\ndeveloping States, as noted in paragraph 5 above;\n\n                                                                                                  7/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18</p>"},{"md":"國家層級之締約方，注意到此類倡議可與執行手段互補並對其有所貢獻，且不應被視為取代所有締約方在《公約》下關於執行手段之義務；","html":"<p>國家層級之締約方，注意到此類倡議可與執行手段互補並對其有所貢獻，且不應被視為取代所有締約方在《公約》下關於執行手段之義務；</p>"},{"md":"19. 亦**歡迎**相關夥伴所提供的支持，包括區域與次區域技術及科學合作支持中心、多邊環境協議秘書處、聯合國實體、政府間組織、非政府組織及研究網絡，以執行綱要，並**邀請**這些夥伴提供進一步支持，特別關注進展仍不夠充分之領域，同時回應締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家[，以及經濟轉型中之締約方,]）所識別之需求與優先事項，包括透過促進同儕學習、南北合作、南南合作及三角合作與資訊交換；","html":"<p>19. 亦<strong>歡迎</strong>相關夥伴所提供的支持，包括區域與次區域技術及科學合作支持中心、多邊環境協議秘書處、聯合國實體、政府間組織、非政府組織及研究網絡，以執行綱要，並<strong>邀請</strong>這些夥伴提供進一步支持，特別關注進展仍不夠充分之領域，同時回應締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家[，以及經濟轉型中之締約方,]）所識別之需求與優先事項，包括透過促進同儕學習、南北合作、南南合作及三角合作與資訊交換；</p>"},{"md":"[19. (提案)\n亦**歡迎**相關夥伴所提供的支持，包括區域與次區域技術及科學合作支持中心、多邊環境協議秘書處、聯合國實體、政府間組織、非政府組織及研究網絡，以執行綱要，並**邀請**這些夥伴提供進一步支持，特別關注進展仍不夠充分之領域，同時回應締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家，以及經濟轉型中之締約方）所識別之需求與優先事項，包括透過促進同儕學習、南北合作、南南合作及三角合作與資訊交換；]","html":"<p>[19. (提案)<br />\n亦<strong>歡迎</strong>相關夥伴所提供的支持，包括區域與次區域技術及科學合作支持中心、多邊環境協議秘書處、聯合國實體、政府間組織、非政府組織及研究網絡，以執行綱要，並<strong>邀請</strong>這些夥伴提供進一步支持，特別關注進展仍不夠充分之領域，同時回應締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家，以及經濟轉型中之締約方）所識別之需求與優先事項，包括透過促進同儕學習、南北合作、南南合作及三角合作與資訊交換；]</p>"},{"md":"擴大範圍與覆蓋範圍","html":"<p>擴大範圍與覆蓋範圍</p>"},{"md":"20. **敦促**尚未完成之締約方盡快完成並提交其修訂或更新之國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及第七次國家報告，並注意到提交國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告乃《公約》第六條與第二十六條下之義務，且在相關且符合國家情況、優先事項與能力之情況下，應考量全球報告中所識別之差距、挑戰、障礙與機會，特別是上述第五段所識別之差距與進展；","html":"<p>20. <strong>敦促</strong>尚未完成之締約方盡快完成並提交其修訂或更新之國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及第七次國家報告，並注意到提交國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫及國家報告乃《公約》第六條與第二十六條下之義務，且在相關且符合國家情況、優先事項與能力之情況下，應考量全球報告中所識別之差距、挑戰、障礙與機會，特別是上述第五段所識別之差距與進展；</p>"},{"md":"21. **鼓勵**尚未進行之締約方，在自願基礎上，透過包容性程序，根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，制定國家生物多樣性財務計畫或類似工具、能力建構及監測計畫，以指導有效執行；","html":"<p>21. <strong>鼓勵</strong>尚未進行之締約方，在自願基礎上，透過包容性程序，根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，制定國家生物多樣性財務計畫或類似工具、能力建構及監測計畫，以指導有效執行；</p>"},{"md":"22. **認可**欲進行之締約方，在符合國家情況、優先事項與能力之情況下，得在適當且相關之處，透過國家工具進一步強化其對執行綱要之承諾，並注意到上述第五段所述之差距，包括關於海洋、沿岸及內陸水域生態系之差距；同時進一步**認可**，締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）能夠執行之程度與速度，取決於是否具備充足、可預測、及時且易於取得之執行手段，且應確保此類發展或修訂不會將注意力或資源從執行綱要之行動中轉移；","html":"<p>22. <strong>認可</strong>欲進行之締約方，在符合國家情況、優先事項與能力之情況下，得在適當且相關之處，透過國家工具進一步強化其對執行綱要之承諾，並注意到上述第五段所述之差距，包括關於海洋、沿岸及內陸水域生態系之差距；同時進一步<strong>認可</strong>，締約方（特別是開發中國家締約方，尤其是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）能夠執行之程度與速度，取決於是否具備充足、可預測、及時且易於取得之執行手段，且應確保此類發展或修訂不會將注意力或資源從執行綱要之行動中轉移；</p>"},{"md":"強化執行手段","html":"<p>強化執行手段</p>"},{"md":"23. **認可**綱要之全面且有效執行，除其他事項外，取決於是否具備充足且及時之執行手段，包括財務資源、能力、技術以及技術與科學資源，且執行手段之差距乃全面且有效執行綱要最常見之障礙，對於開發中國家締約方（特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）而言尤甚，如上述第五段所述；","html":"<p>23. <strong>認可</strong>綱要之全面且有效執行，除其他事項外，取決於是否具備充足且及時之執行手段，包括財務資源、能力、技術以及技術與科學資源，且執行手段之差距乃全面且有效執行綱要最常見之障礙，對於開發中國家締約方（特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）而言尤甚，如上述第五段所述；</p>"}]},{"p":"p008","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p008.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n             24. Invites Parties, on a voluntary basis and as appropriate, to identify specific priorities to\n       implement their national biodiversity strategies and action plans and corresponding necessary means\n       of implementation, and to reflect those needs in relevant plans and policies and share that\n       information, where relevant and appropriate;\n             25. Encourages Parties, in accordance with national circumstances, priorities and\n       capabilities, to mobilize domestic and international public and private finance for biodiversity in line\n       with a view to filling the gaps identified in paragraph 5 above;\n             [26. Also encourages Parties, in accordance with national circumstances, priorities and\n       capabilities, to accelerate the identification, as well as [of] elimination, phasing out or reform of\n       [negative] incentives, including subsidies, harmful to biodiversity and [the identification and] the\n       scaling up of positive incentives, so as to bridge the gaps identified in paragraph 5 above;]\n              [26. (proposal)       Also encourages Parties, in accordance with national circumstances,\n       priorities and capabilities, to accelerate the implementation of Target 18 of the Framework;]\n              27. Strongly encourages developed country Parties, and invites other Parties in a position\n       to do so on a voluntary basis, as well as other Governments, multilateral and bilateral agencies and\n       funds and other relevant organizations, to increase their support, including through the provision and\n       mobilization of adequate, predictable and timely means of implementation in an easily accessible\n       manner, such as simplified access procedures and strengthened country ownership of supported\n       activities, and to continue to provide transparent information on the resources provided to developing\n       country Parties, in particular the least developed countries and the small island developing States,\n       and Parties with economies in transition, thereby contributing to addressing the gaps identified in\n       paragraph 5 above;\n              28. Requests the Global Environment Facility to enhance the provision of adequate,\n       predictable, timely and transparent means of implementation in an easily accessible manner to\n       developing country Parties, in particular the least developed countries and the small island\n       developing States, and Parties with economies in transition, including by simplifying access\n       procedures and strengthening country ownership of activities to support the Framework, in\n       accordance with national biodiversity strategies and action plans, and to share through existing\n       reporting arrangements, and without creating additional burdens, detailed information on the\n       resources provided and how they address the needs and priorities identified by Parties through their\n       projects, without prejudice to the process established under decision[s] 16/34 of 27 February 2025\n       [and 17/--] on resource mobilization;\n             29. Also requests the Global Environment Facility and its implementing agencies, including\n       the United Nations Development Programme in the context of the Biodiversity Finance Initiative, to\n       expedite the support to all eligible Parties on their enabling support activities for the development of\n       biodiversity finance plans, in order to unlock financial resources for the implementation of national\n       biodiversity strategies and action plans, as described in decision 15/7 of 19 December 2022;\n             30. Encourages Parties, and invites other Governments, multilateral and bilateral agencies\n       and funds and other organizations, to facilitate access to means of implementation for indigenous\n       peoples and local communities, women and youth;\n               31. Invites relevant organizations, including the technical and scientific cooperation support\n       centres, to continue to provide support for strengthening capacity-building and development\n       initiatives and addressing gaps and challenges at the national and subnational levels;\n       Engaging the whole of government\n              32. Invites Parties, in accordance with their national circumstances and priorities, to\n       strengthen the whole-of-government-approach to implementation, including by strengthening\n       institutional, inter-institutional, policy, legal, fiscal, budgetary and regulatory coordination\n\n\n8/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18</p>"},{"md":"24. **邀請**締約方在自願且適當的情況下，識別執行其國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫之特定優先事項及相應之必要執行手段，並在相關計畫與政策中反映該等需求，且在相關且適當時分享該等資訊；\n25. **鼓勵**締約方根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，為生物多樣性動員國內及國際之公共與私人資金，以期填補上述第 5 段所識別之差距；\n[26. **亦鼓勵**締約方根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，加速識別以及[消除]、逐步淘汰或改革對生物多樣性有害之[負面]誘因（包括補貼），並[識別及]擴大正面誘因，以彌補上述第 5 段所識別之差距；]\n[26.（提案）\n**亦鼓勵**締約方根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，加速執行綱要之目標 18；]\n27. **強烈鼓勵**已開發國家締約方，並**邀請**有能力且在自願基礎上之其他締約方，以及其他政府、多邊與雙邊機構及基金與其他相關組織，增加其支持，包括透過以易於取得之方式提供及動員充足、可預測且及時之執行手段，例如簡化取得程序及強化受支持活動之國家自主權，並持續提供關於提供予開發中國家締約方（特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）及經濟轉型中締約方之資源的透明資訊，從而有助於解決上述第 5 段所識別之差距；\n28. **請求**全球環境基金以易於取得之方式，加強向開發中國家締約方（特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）及經濟轉型中締約方提供充足、可預測、及時且透明之執行手段，包括透過簡化取得程序及強化支持綱要之活動的國家自主權（以符合國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫），並透過現有報告安排，且在不增加額外負擔的情況下，分享關於所提供資源之詳細資訊，以及該等資源如何透過其專案解決締約方所識別之需求與優先事項，且不損害根據 2025 年 2 月 27 日決議[s] 16/34 [及 17/--] 就資源動員所建立之程序；\n29. **亦請求**全球環境基金及其執行機構（包括在生物多樣性金融倡議背景下之聯合國開發計畫），加速對所有符合資格之締約方在開發生物多樣性金融計畫之賦能支持活動方面之支持，以釋放用於執行國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫之財務資源，如 2022 年 12 月 19 日決議 15/7 所述；\n30. **鼓勵**締約方，並**邀請**其他政府、多邊與雙邊機構及基金與其他組織，促進原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年取得執行手段；\n31. **邀請**相關組織（包括技術與科學合作支持中心），繼續提供支持以強化能力建構與發展倡議，並應對國家與次國家級層級之差距與挑戰；","html":"<p>24. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方在自願且適當的情況下，識別執行其國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫之特定優先事項及相應之必要執行手段，並在相關計畫與政策中反映該等需求，且在相關且適當時分享該等資訊；<br />\n25. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，為生物多樣性動員國內及國際之公共與私人資金，以期填補上述第 5 段所識別之差距；<br />\n[26. <strong>亦鼓勵</strong>締約方根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，加速識別以及[消除]、逐步淘汰或改革對生物多樣性有害之[負面]誘因（包括補貼），並[識別及]擴大正面誘因，以彌補上述第 5 段所識別之差距；]<br />\n[26.（提案）<br />\n<strong>亦鼓勵</strong>締約方根據國家情況、優先事項與能力，加速執行綱要之目標 18；]<br />\n27. <strong>強烈鼓勵</strong>已開發國家締約方，並<strong>邀請</strong>有能力且在自願基礎上之其他締約方，以及其他政府、多邊與雙邊機構及基金與其他相關組織，增加其支持，包括透過以易於取得之方式提供及動員充足、可預測且及時之執行手段，例如簡化取得程序及強化受支持活動之國家自主權，並持續提供關於提供予開發中國家締約方（特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）及經濟轉型中締約方之資源的透明資訊，從而有助於解決上述第 5 段所識別之差距；<br />\n28. <strong>請求</strong>全球環境基金以易於取得之方式，加強向開發中國家締約方（特別是低度開發國家與小島嶼開發中國家）及經濟轉型中締約方提供充足、可預測、及時且透明之執行手段，包括透過簡化取得程序及強化支持綱要之活動的國家自主權（以符合國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫），並透過現有報告安排，且在不增加額外負擔的情況下，分享關於所提供資源之詳細資訊，以及該等資源如何透過其專案解決締約方所識別之需求與優先事項，且不損害根據 2025 年 2 月 27 日決議[s] 16/34 [及 17/--] 就資源動員所建立之程序；<br />\n29. <strong>亦請求</strong>全球環境基金及其執行機構（包括在生物多樣性金融倡議背景下之聯合國開發計畫），加速對所有符合資格之締約方在開發生物多樣性金融計畫之賦能支持活動方面之支持，以釋放用於執行國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫之財務資源，如 2022 年 12 月 19 日決議 15/7 所述；<br />\n30. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，並<strong>邀請</strong>其他政府、多邊與雙邊機構及基金與其他組織，促進原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年取得執行手段；<br />\n31. <strong>邀請</strong>相關組織（包括技術與科學合作支持中心），繼續提供支持以強化能力建構與發展倡議，並應對國家與次國家級層級之差距與挑戰；</p>"},{"md":"推動全政府參與\n32. **邀請**締約方根據其國家情況與優先事項，強化執行之全政府方法，包括透過強化制度、跨制度、政策、法律、財政、預算與監管協調","html":"<p>推動全政府參與<br />\n32. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方根據其國家情況與優先事項，強化執行之全政府方法，包括透過強化制度、跨制度、政策、法律、財政、預算與監管協調</p>"}]},{"p":"p009","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p009.md","en":"                                                                              CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\nmechanisms, integrated planning, monitoring and reporting processes, and mainstreaming\nbiodiversity and its multiple values across and within sectors;\n       33. Encourages Parties, in accordance with their national circumstances and priorities, to\npromote policy coherence across biodiversity and other areas, including climate, food, water, health,\nland-use and sustainable development, in, inter alia, the implementation of national strategies and\npolicies developed under the Rio conventions and other biodiversity-related multilateral environment\nagreements;\n       34. Invites Parties to integrate commitments and actions in support of the implementation\nof the Framework from subnational and local governments and different sectors of government in\ntheir national biodiversity strategies and action plans and in their national targets and national reports,\nas appropriate and where relevant;\nEngaging the whole of society\n      35. Emphasizes that actions from indigenous peoples and local communities, subnational\ngovernments, cities and other local authorities, intergovernmental organizations, other multilateral\nenvironment agreements, non-governmental organizations, women, youth, research organizations,\nthe business and finance community and representatives of sectors related to or dependent on\nbiodiversity contribute to the implementation of the Framework, including through national\nbiodiversity strategies and action plans, and that a more effective use of their contributions,\nknowledge, expertise and initiatives could represent a significant opportunity to accelerate the\nimplementation of the Framework;\n     36. Encourages Parties to strengthen the engagement of all actors other than national\nGovernments, in particular indigenous peoples and local communities, women and youth, including\nby enabling their full, equitable, inclusive, effective and gender-responsive participation and\nengagement, in order to ensure that all actors are involved in consultation processes, planning and\nimplementation, in accordance with national legislation;\n      37. Invites actors other than national Governments to share information with Parties on their\nactions in relation to the implementation of the Framework;\n       38. Invites Parties, to reflect commitments and actions in support of the implementation of\nthe Framework by actors other than national Governments, in particular indigenous peoples and local\ncommunities, women and youth, in accordance with their national circumstances and priorities, in\ntheir national biodiversity strategies and action plans and national targets and national reports, as\nappropriate and where relevant;\nEnhancing monitoring and knowledge\n       39. Urges Parties to further develop or strengthen national monitoring and knowledge\nmanagement systems to address the gaps in data, including national ecosystem data and spatial\ninformation, and knowledge mentioned above and to improve evidenced-based decision-making and\nthe tracking of national progress in the implementation of the Framework, inter alia, to enable the\noperationalization of the ecosystem-based headline indicators;\n      40. Invites other Governments and relevant organizations, and requests the Global\nEnvironment Facility, to continue to support the development and strengthening of national\nmonitoring and knowledge management systems, including national ecosystem data and spatial\ninformation, inter alia, to enable the operationalization of the ecosystem-based headline indicators;\n       41. Invites actors other than national Governments, the technical and scientific cooperation\nsupport centres, the secretariats of biodiversity-related conventions and other relevant multilateral\nenvironmental agreements, United Nations entities and intergovernmental organizations to support\nthe efforts of Parties to improve national monitoring and knowledge management systems;\n\n\n\n                                                                                                      9/11","blocks":[{"md":"機制、整合性規劃、監測與報告流程，以及在各部門之間及部門內部將生物多樣性及其多元價值主流化；","html":"<p>機制、整合性規劃、監測與報告流程，以及在各部門之間及部門內部將生物多樣性及其多元價值主流化；</p>"},{"md":"33. **鼓勵**締約方根據其國情與優先事項，在執行根據里約協定及其他與生物多樣性相關之多邊環境協定所制定之國家策略與政策時，於其他方面促進生物多樣性與其他領域（包括氣候、糧食、水、健康、土地利用及永續發展）之間的政策一致性；","html":"<p>33. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方根據其國情與優先事項，在執行根據里約協定及其他與生物多樣性相關之多邊環境協定所制定之國家策略與政策時，於其他方面促進生物多樣性與其他領域（包括氣候、糧食、水、健康、土地利用及永續發展）之間的政策一致性；</p>"},{"md":"34. **邀請**締約方在適當且相關之情況下，於其國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫以及國家目標與國家報告中，整合來自次國家級政府、地方政府及政府不同部門支持執行綱要之承諾與行動；","html":"<p>34. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方在適當且相關之情況下，於其國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫以及國家目標與國家報告中，整合來自次國家級政府、地方政府及政府不同部門支持執行綱要之承諾與行動；</p>"},{"md":"**參與全社會**","html":"<p><strong>參與全社會</strong></p>"},{"md":"35. **強調**原住民族與地方社區、次國家級政府、城市及其他地方當局、政府間組織、其他多邊環境協定、非政府組織、女性、青年、研究機構、企業與金融界，以及與生物多樣性相關或依賴生物多樣性之部門代表，其行動（包括透過國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫）皆有助於執行綱要，且更有效地利用其貢獻、知識、專業知識與倡議，可望成為加速執行綱要之重大契機；","html":"<p>35. <strong>強調</strong>原住民族與地方社區、次國家級政府、城市及其他地方當局、政府間組織、其他多邊環境協定、非政府組織、女性、青年、研究機構、企業與金融界，以及與生物多樣性相關或依賴生物多樣性之部門代表，其行動（包括透過國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫）皆有助於執行綱要，且更有效地利用其貢獻、知識、專業知識與倡議，可望成為加速執行綱要之重大契機；</p>"},{"md":"36. **鼓勵**締約方加強國家政府以外所有參與者的參與，特別是原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年，包括透過使其能夠充分、公平、包容、有效且具性別回應之參與及投入，以確保所有參與者皆能根據國家立法參與諮詢、規劃與執行程序；","html":"<p>36. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方加強國家政府以外所有參與者的參與，特別是原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年，包括透過使其能夠充分、公平、包容、有效且具性別回應之參與及投入，以確保所有參與者皆能根據國家立法參與諮詢、規劃與執行程序；</p>"},{"md":"37. **邀請**國家政府以外之參與者，與締約方分享其關於執行綱要行動之資訊；","html":"<p>37. <strong>邀請</strong>國家政府以外之參與者，與締約方分享其關於執行綱要行動之資訊；</p>"},{"md":"38. **邀請**締約方在適當且相關之情況下，於其國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標與國家報告中，根據其國情與優先事項，反映國家政府以外之參與者（特別是原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年）支持執行綱要之承諾與行動；","html":"<p>38. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方在適當且相關之情況下，於其國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標與國家報告中，根據其國情與優先事項，反映國家政府以外之參與者（特別是原住民族與地方社區、女性與青年）支持執行綱要之承諾與行動；</p>"},{"md":"**強化監測與知識**","html":"<p><strong>強化監測與知識</strong></p>"},{"md":"39. **敦促**締約方進一步開發或強化國家監測與知識管理系統，以解決資料（包括國家生態系資料與空間資訊）及知識之缺口，並改善以證據為基礎之決策，以及追蹤執行綱要之國家進度，其中包括使以生態系為基礎之主要指標得以落實；","html":"<p>39. <strong>敦促</strong>締約方進一步開發或強化國家監測與知識管理系統，以解決資料（包括國家生態系資料與空間資訊）及知識之缺口，並改善以證據為基礎之決策，以及追蹤執行綱要之國家進度，其中包括使以生態系為基礎之主要指標得以落實；</p>"},{"md":"40. **邀請**其他政府與相關組織，並**請求**全球環境基金，繼續支持國家監測與知識管理系統（包括國家生態系資料與空間資訊）之開發與強化，其中包括使以生態系為基礎之主要指標得以落實；","html":"<p>40. <strong>邀請</strong>其他政府與相關組織，並<strong>請求</strong>全球環境基金，繼續支持國家監測與知識管理系統（包括國家生態系資料與空間資訊）之開發與強化，其中包括使以生態系為基礎之主要指標得以落實；</p>"},{"md":"41. **邀請**國家政府以外之參與者、技術與科學合作支援中心、生物多樣性相關公約及其他相關多邊環境協定之秘書處、聯合國實體及政府間組織，支持締約方改善國家監測與知識管理系統之努力；","html":"<p>41. <strong>邀請</strong>國家政府以外之參與者、技術與科學合作支援中心、生物多樣性相關公約及其他相關多邊環境協定之秘書處、聯合國實體及政府間組織，支持締約方改善國家監測與知識管理系統之努力；</p>"}]},{"p":"p010","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p010.md","en":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n               42. Encourages Parties to make full use of existing mechanisms, tools, the technical and\n        scientific cooperation support centres, capacity-building and development initiatives and plans, and\n        enhanced regional cooperation to strengthen monitoring and knowledge management systems;\n               43. Invites Parties, other Governments, actors other than national Governments and the\n        technical and scientific cooperation support centres supporting the implementation of the Framework\n        to make use of the information that is contained in the global report and the national biodiversity\n        strategies and action plans, national targets and national reports, when executing their respective\n        mandates, and that is within their area of competence, where relevant;\n              44. Encourages Parties and actors other than national Governments to share experiences,\n        lessons learned and best practices in the implementation of the Framework, including by uploading\n        them to the virtual library of the clearing-house mechanism, and invites Parties to make use of that\n        information, where relevant;\n              [45. Invites Parties to engage in regional and subregional dialogues on the global review and\n        the Yerevan Pathway for accelerating implementation of the Framework[[, in line with paragraph 20\n        of decision 15/6] [, with a view to sharing actions and efforts, as appropriate][and share [any][their]\n        respective actions and efforts, as appropriate;]]]\n              [45.Alt.       Invites Parties to engage in regional and subregional dialogues for the\n        acceleration of the implementation of the Framework, informed by the Yerevan Pathway, with a view\n        to sharing experiences, best practices, challenges and barriers in enhancing actions and efforts, as\n        appropriate;]\n              [45. (proposal)     Invites Parties to engage in regional and subregional dialogues on the\n        global review and the Yerevan Pathway for accelerating the implementation of the Framework, with\n        a view to sharing experiences, best practices, challenges and barriers in enhancing actions and efforts,\n        as appropriate;]\n              46. Invites relevant partner organizations, in particular, the technical and scientific\n        cooperation support centres, in coordination with the Secretariat, to conduct workshops or dialogues,\n        building on existing processes, as appropriate, on the findings of the first global review, with a view\n        to promoting peer-to-peer learning and sharing experiences and best practices;\n              47. Invites the United Nations Environment Programme to organize a fourth Bern\n        Conference on cooperation among multilateral environmental agreements with a view to enhancing\n        the implementation of the Framework by multilateral environmental agreements, based on the\n        findings of the global report and global review and to providing options to better align planning,\n        monitoring and reporting obligations under the Convention and those under other relevant processes;\n              48. Encourages Parties, and invites other Governments and relevant organizations to\n        support the reporting by indigenous peoples and local communities on the implementation of the\n        Framework including by continuing to support community-based monitoring and information\n        systems and initiatives such as the Local Biodiversity Outlook;\n              49.    Requests the Executive Secretary:\n              (a) To take into account the present decision and the gaps, challenges, barriers and\n        opportunities identified in the global report in the review of the programmes of work;\n               (b) To facilitate efforts by Parties and actors other than national Governments to share\n        experiences, lessons learned and best practices in the implementation of the Framework, including\n        on actions taken in follow-up to the global review, including through the virtual library of the\n        clearing-house mechanism, while promoting North-South, South-South and triangular cooperation;\n              (c) To provide a summary of the information submitted pursuant to subparagraph 49 (b)\n        above at a meeting of the Subsidiary Body on Implementation held before the eighteenth meeting of\n        the conference of the Parties, subject to the availability of resources;\n\n10/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18</p>"},{"md":"42. **鼓勵**締約方充分利用現有機制、工具、技術與科學合作支援中心、能力建構與發展倡議及計畫，以及強化區域合作，以強化監測與知識管理系統；\n43. **邀請**締約方、其他政府、國家政府以外之行動者，以及支援綱要實施之技術與科學合作支援中心，在執行其各自職責且在相關之權責範圍內時，利用全球報告、國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及國家報告中所包含之資訊；\n44. **鼓勵**締約方與國家政府以外之行動者分享實施綱要之經驗、經驗教訓與最佳實務，包括將其上傳至資訊交換機制之虛擬圖書館，並**邀請**締約方在相關情況下利用該等資訊；\n[45. **邀請**締約方參與關於全球審查與加速實施綱要之葉里溫路徑的區域與次區域對話[[，與決議 15/6 第 20 段一致][，以適當地分享行動與努力][並在適當情況下分享[任何][各自的]行動與努力；]] ]\n[45. Alt.\n**邀請**締約方參與旨在加速綱要實施之區域與次區域對話，並以葉里溫路徑為參考，以在適當情況下分享增強行動與努力之經驗、最佳實務、挑戰與障礙；]\n[45. (proposal)\n**邀請**締約方參與關於全球審查與加速實施綱要之葉里溫路徑的區域與次區域對話，以在適當情況下分享增強行動與努力之經驗、最佳實務、挑戰與障礙；]\n46. **邀請**相關夥伴組織，特別是技術與科學合作支援中心，與秘書處協調，在適當情況下基於現有程序，針對第一次全球審查之結果舉辦工作坊或對話，以促進同儕學習並分享經驗與最佳實務；\n47. **邀請**聯合國環境規劃署舉辦第四屆伯恩會議，以促進各項多邊環境協議之間之合作，旨在根據全球報告與全球審查之結果，透過多邊環境協議強化綱要之實施，並提供選項以使公約下之規劃、監測與報告義務，與其他相關程序下之義務能更佳地接軌；\n48. **鼓勵**締約方，並**邀請**其他政府與相關組織，支持原住民族與地方社區就綱要實施情況進行報告，包括繼續支持以社區為基礎的監測與資訊系統及倡議，例如地方生物多樣性展望；\n49. **請求**執行秘書：","html":"<p>42. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方充分利用現有機制、工具、技術與科學合作支援中心、能力建構與發展倡議及計畫，以及強化區域合作，以強化監測與知識管理系統；<br />\n43. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方、其他政府、國家政府以外之行動者，以及支援綱要實施之技術與科學合作支援中心，在執行其各自職責且在相關之權責範圍內時，利用全球報告、國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標及國家報告中所包含之資訊；<br />\n44. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方與國家政府以外之行動者分享實施綱要之經驗、經驗教訓與最佳實務，包括將其上傳至資訊交換機制之虛擬圖書館，並<strong>邀請</strong>締約方在相關情況下利用該等資訊；<br />\n[45. <strong>邀請</strong>締約方參與關於全球審查與加速實施綱要之葉里溫路徑的區域與次區域對話[[，與決議 15/6 第 20 段一致][，以適當地分享行動與努力][並在適當情況下分享[任何][各自的]行動與努力；]] ]<br />\n[45. Alt.<br />\n<strong>邀請</strong>締約方參與旨在加速綱要實施之區域與次區域對話，並以葉里溫路徑為參考，以在適當情況下分享增強行動與努力之經驗、最佳實務、挑戰與障礙；]<br />\n[45. (proposal)<br />\n<strong>邀請</strong>締約方參與關於全球審查與加速實施綱要之葉里溫路徑的區域與次區域對話，以在適當情況下分享增強行動與努力之經驗、最佳實務、挑戰與障礙；]<br />\n46. <strong>邀請</strong>相關夥伴組織，特別是技術與科學合作支援中心，與秘書處協調，在適當情況下基於現有程序，針對第一次全球審查之結果舉辦工作坊或對話，以促進同儕學習並分享經驗與最佳實務；<br />\n47. <strong>邀請</strong>聯合國環境規劃署舉辦第四屆伯恩會議，以促進各項多邊環境協議之間之合作，旨在根據全球報告與全球審查之結果，透過多邊環境協議強化綱要之實施，並提供選項以使公約下之規劃、監測與報告義務，與其他相關程序下之義務能更佳地接軌；<br />\n48. <strong>鼓勵</strong>締約方，並<strong>邀請</strong>其他政府與相關組織，支持原住民族與地方社區就綱要實施情況進行報告，包括繼續支持以社區為基礎的監測與資訊系統及倡議，例如地方生物多樣性展望；<br />\n49. <strong>請求</strong>執行秘書：</p>"},{"md":"(a) 在審查工作計畫時，應考慮本決議以及全球報告中所識別之差距、挑戰、障礙與機會；\n(b) 促進締約方與國家政府以外之行動者分享實施綱要之經驗、經驗教訓與最佳實務之努力，包括針對全球審查後續所採取之行動，包括透過資訊交換機制之虛擬圖書館，同時促進南北合作、南南合作與三角合作；\n(c) 在第十八次締約方大會召開前召開之執行附屬機構會議上，在資源可用之情況下，提供根據上述第 49 (b) 小段所提交資訊之摘要；","html":"<p>(a) 在審查工作計畫時，應考慮本決議以及全球報告中所識別之差距、挑戰、障礙與機會；<br />\n(b) 促進締約方與國家政府以外之行動者分享實施綱要之經驗、經驗教訓與最佳實務之努力，包括針對全球審查後續所採取之行動，包括透過資訊交換機制之虛擬圖書館，同時促進南北合作、南南合作與三角合作；<br />\n(c) 在第十八次締約方大會召開前召開之執行附屬機構會議上，在資源可用之情況下，提供根據上述第 49 (b) 小段所提交資訊之摘要；</p>"}]},{"p":"p011","zh":"CBD_documents/zh/6ae34a076aaa456609231d8e/p011.md","en":"                                                                                   CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18\n\n              (d) To continue to coordinate with partner organizations, including the technical and\n       scientific cooperation support centres, the facilitation of peer-to-peer learning and capacity-building\n       activities, subject to the availability of resources;\n             [(e) To facilitate, in coordination with Parties, the technical and scientific cooperation\n       support centres and relevant stakeholders, the holding of subregional and regional dialogues on the\n       global review and the Yerevan Pathway for accelerating the implementation of the Framework and\n       to compile views from Parties and stakeholders for the preparation of such dialogues, subject to the\n       availability of resources;]\n             [(e.proposal) To facilitate, in coordination with Parties, the technical and scientific\n       cooperation support centres and relevant stakeholders, the holding of subregional and regional\n       dialogues on the global review and the Yerevan Pathway for accelerating the implementation of the\n       Framework and to compile views from Parties and stakeholders for the preparation of such dialogues,\n       subject to the availability of resources;]\n             (f)    To make the information contained in the submitted national biodiversity strategies and\n       action plans, national targets, national reports and commitments from actors other than national\n       Governments more easily accessible;\n             (g) To make the findings from the global report easily accessible, including through the\n       development of information dashboards on the clearing-house mechanism of the Convention on\n       Biological Diversity;11\n             (h) To continue to coordinate and collaborate with relevant partners to provide capacity-\n       building and development support to Parties and actors other than national Governments aimed at\n       improving national monitoring and knowledge management systems.\n                                                          __________\n\n\n\n\n11 United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1760, No. 30619.\n\n\n\n                                                                                                        11/11","blocks":[{"md":"CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18","html":"<p>CBD/COP/17/WG.I/CRP.18</p>"},{"md":"(d) 在資源可用的情況下，繼續與夥伴組織（包括技術與科學合作支援中心）協調，促進同儕學習與能力建構活動；","html":"<p>(d) 在資源可用的情況下，繼續與夥伴組織（包括技術與科學合作支援中心）協調，促進同儕學習與能力建構活動；</p>"},{"md":"[(e) 在資源可用的情況下，與締約方、技術與科學合作支援中心及相關利害關係人協調，促進針對全球審查及「葉里溫路徑」（Yerevan Pathway）舉行次區域與區域對話，以加速綱要的實施，並彙整締約方與利害關係人之意見，以利籌備此類對話；]","html":"<p>[(e) 在資源可用的情況下，與締約方、技術與科學合作支援中心及相關利害關係人協調，促進針對全球審查及「葉里溫路徑」（Yerevan Pathway）舉行次區域與區域對話，以加速綱要的實施，並彙整締約方與利害關係人之意見，以利籌備此類對話；]</p>"},{"md":"[(e.提案) 在資源可用的情況下，與締約方、技術與科學合作支援中心及相關利害關係人協調，促進針對全球審查及「葉里溫路徑」（Yerevan Pathway）舉行次區域與區域對話，以加速綱要的實施，並彙整締約方與利害關係人之意見，以利籌備此類對話；]","html":"<p>[(e.提案) 在資源可用的情況下，與締約方、技術與科學合作支援中心及相關利害關係人協調，促進針對全球審查及「葉里溫路徑」（Yerevan Pathway）舉行次區域與區域對話，以加速綱要的實施，並彙整締約方與利害關係人之意見，以利籌備此類對話；]</p>"},{"md":"(f) 使提交之國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標、國家報告，以及來自國家政府以外之行動者所承諾之資訊，更易於取得；","html":"<p>(f) 使提交之國家生物多樣性策略與行動計畫、國家目標、國家報告，以及來自國家政府以外之行動者所承諾之資訊，更易於取得；</p>"},{"md":"(g) 使全球報告之調查結果更易於取得，包括透過在生物多樣性公約資訊交換機制上開發資訊儀表板之方式；¹¹","html":"<p>(g) 使全球報告之調查結果更易於取得，包括透過在生物多樣性公約資訊交換機制上開發資訊儀表板之方式；¹¹</p>"},{"md":"(h) 繼續與相關夥伴協調與合作，為締約方及國家政府以外之行動者提供能力建構與發展支援，旨在改善國家監測與知識管理系統。\n__________","html":"<p>(h) 繼續與相關夥伴協調與合作，為締約方及國家政府以外之行動者提供能力建構與發展支援，旨在改善國家監測與知識管理系統。</p>\n<hr />"},{"md":"¹¹ United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1760, No. 30619.","html":"<p>¹¹ United Nations, Treaty Series, vol. 1760, No. 30619.</p>"}]}]};
